+# Epic 2 Retrospective: Trustworthy Live Conditions
+
+Date: 2026-03-19
+Epic: 2
+Project: bmad-6-workshop-migration
+Retrospective Type: Epic completion review
+Previous Retrospective: None found for Epic 1
+Epic Update Required: No major rewrite required
+
+## Epic Summary
+
+Epic 2 delivered the live trust layer for the Royal Institution display without breaking the calm public shell established in Epic 1.
+
+- Completed stories: 5/5
+- Sprint status at review: `epic-2: done`
+- Retrospective status: completed by this artifact
+- Story sequence completed:
+  - 2.1 live dashboard read path and snapshot orchestration
+  - 2.2 trend and freshness confidence modeling
+  - 2.3 composed disruption emphasis
+  - 2.4 localized provider-failure handling
+  - 2.5 stable live reading and reduced-motion-safe updates
+
+## Participants
+
+- Workshop, Project Lead
+- Bob, Scrum Master
+- John, Product Manager
+- Amelia, Developer
+- Quinn, QA Engineer
+- Winston, Architect
+
+## Delivery Metrics
+
+- Completion: 5 of 5 stories marked `done`
+- Story tracking discipline: all Epic 2 implementation stories are recorded in `docs/sprint-artifacts`
+- Validation evidence: every Epic 2 story records successful validation through `npm run validate`
+- Review closure: story records show 16 review findings resolved across implementation and follow-up review cycles
+- Deployment evidence: repository validation is strong; separate venue deployment acceptance is not yet captured in repo artifacts
+
+## What Went Well
+
+- The team extended one canonical dashboard model instead of creating parallel UI-specific state paths. That kept the public route coherent across live data, trust, disruption, degradation, and update continuity work.
+- Each story built on the previous one with disciplined scope boundaries. Epic 2 did not collapse into an uncontrolled rewrite of Epic 1.
+- The review loop was effective. High-severity issues around runtime truthfulness, fallback honesty, and live-update semantics were caught and fixed before the epic closed.
+- Automated coverage matured in the right places: unit, live-path, and smoke tests now protect behavior that matters to a calm public display rather than only field presence.
+- Verification artifacts improved. Story 2.5 added explicit live-reading notes instead of treating venue-scale and reduced-motion behavior as implied.
+
+## Challenges And Growth Areas
+
+- Boundary truth was the hardest recurring problem. Several fixes were needed because fallback, fixture, or compatibility paths initially overstated certainty.
+- Public calmness is easy to regress accidentally. The team repeatedly had to correct wording, labels, and update summaries that drifted toward implementation truth without fully matching visitor-facing truth.
+- Artifact synchronization lagged behind code in a few places. Story records, file lists, and sprint tracking needed cleanup after review.
+- The repo still lacks a prior-retrospective habit. Epic 1 closed without a saved retrospective, so Epic 2 had no continuity baseline to measure against.
+
+## Story-Level Patterns
+
+### Common Success Pattern
+
+The strongest implementation pattern was:
+
+1. derive meaning in server or contract layers
+2. shape it once in the presenter
+3. keep public components thin
+4. lock the behavior with targeted tests
+
+That pattern held through all five stories and is a repeatable strength for Epic 3.
+
+### Common Struggle Pattern
+
+The most common struggle was honest negative-path modeling. Review findings repeatedly surfaced the same category of risk:
+
+- static or fallback behavior appearing more current than reality
+- synthetic trend or trust signals without evidence
+- unavailable data inheriting live-state semantics
+- UI summaries describing current state instead of actual change deltas
+
+This was not a people problem. It was a systems problem: the product’s hardest work lives in preserving truthfulness when live evidence is incomplete.
+
+## Key Insights
+
+1. Calm public displays need stricter truth rules than typical dashboards. If live evidence weakens, the UI must narrow confidence immediately and locally.
+2. Fallback behavior is product behavior, not implementation plumbing. The fallback path needs the same design rigor and test coverage as the happy path.
+3. Presenter-level semantics are paying off. The more meaning stays canonical, the less risk there is of accidental drift between stories.
+4. Epic 3 should inherit Epic 2’s readiness and source-status model directly. A second ops-only truth model would create avoidable divergence.
+5. Retrospective hygiene itself is now a process gap to close. The project needs saved retrospectives at epic boundaries so lessons compound instead of resetting.
+
+## Previous Retrospective Follow-Through
+
+No Epic 1 retrospective artifact was found in `docs/sprint-artifacts`, so there were no prior action items to audit.
+
+Implication:
+
+- this is the first durable retrospective baseline for the project
+- Epic 3 should use this document as the continuity reference
+
+## Next Epic Preview
+
+Next epic: Epic 3, `Venue Operations and Public Reliability`
+
+Epic 3 is well aligned with what Epic 2 delivered. The stories map cleanly onto newly completed capabilities:
+
+- Story 3.1 depends on the existing separation between public behavior and staff-only operational behavior
+- Story 3.2 depends on the current readiness, trust, and source-status semantics
+- Story 3.3 depends on localized degraded-impact modeling completed in Story 2.4
+- Story 3.4 depends on the existing refresh and trust-check architecture without leaking controls to the public route
+- Story 3.5 depends on file-backed last-safe snapshot behavior and truthful recovery labeling
+
+## Significant Discoveries
+
+No major planning rewrite is required for Epic 3, but three implementation truths should be treated as binding:
+
+- Epic 3 must reuse the existing canonical status and trust model instead of inventing a separate operational taxonomy.
+- Recovery and ops flows must be tested against real degraded and unavailable paths, not only successful live snapshots.
+- Local-only access control and public-route isolation need to be explicit from the first Epic 3 story, because the repo now contains enough operational behavior that accidental leakage would be high impact.
+
+## Action Items
+
+### Process Improvements
+
+1. Add a boundary-truth checklist to Epic 3 story reviews.
+   - Owner: Bob
+   - Deadline: before any Epic 3 story is marked `review`
+   - Success criteria: each review checks runtime truth, fallback honesty, and public-copy accuracy explicitly
+
+2. Make artifact sync part of the Epic 3 definition of done.
+   - Owner: Bob
+   - Deadline: before any Epic 3 story is marked `done`
+   - Success criteria: story status, sprint status, file list, and review outcome all match the final implementation state
+
+### Technical Improvements
+
+3. Reuse one canonical readiness model for both public and ops surfaces.
+   - Owner: Winston and Amelia
+   - Deadline: before Story 3.2 implementation starts
+   - Success criteria: Epic 3 introduces no parallel readiness taxonomy outside the existing dashboard and source-status contracts
+
+4. Treat negative-path coverage as mandatory for every Epic 3 story.
+   - Owner: Quinn and Amelia
+   - Deadline: starting with Story 3.1
+   - Success criteria: every Epic 3 story adds at least one regression test for degraded, denied, unavailable, or recovery behavior
+
+### Team Agreements
+
+- No fixture, fallback, or carried-forward path is allowed to imply stronger certainty than live evidence supports.
+- Any new ops behavior must remain clearly separated from the public route from the first implementation step.
+- Retrospective artifacts must be created at epic completion so continuity is preserved.
+
+## Preparation Tasks For Epic 3
+
+- Define the local-only ops access rule set and denial behavior before Story 3.1 implementation.
+- Write the shared readiness vocabulary for `current`, `reduced-confidence`, and `unavailable` so Story 3.2 and Story 3.3 stay aligned with Epic 2 semantics.
+- Map current snapshot persistence and recovery behavior to Story 3.5 acceptance criteria before recovery work begins.
+- Decide which Epic 2 verification assets can be reused for ops validation and which need new coverage.
+
+## Critical Path
+
+1. Lock local-only access assumptions before building the ops surface.
+   - Owner: Winston
+
+2. Freeze the shared readiness and degraded-impact vocabulary before Story 3.2 and Story 3.3 branch into implementation.
+   - Owner: Winston and Bob
+
+3. Confirm recovery behavior against the existing snapshot-store and fallback model before Story 3.5 is implemented.
+   - Owner: Amelia and Quinn
+
+## Readiness Assessment
+
+### Testing And Quality
+
+Status: strong
+
+Evidence:
+
+- Epic 2 story records consistently cite `npm run validate`
+- unit, live-path, and smoke coverage were extended through the epic
+- Story 2.5 added explicit verification notes for stable live reading
+
+### Deployment And Release
+
+Status: partially evidenced
+
+Evidence:
+
+- repository validation is complete
+- venue-style verification notes exist
+- a separate deployment or public-release acceptance artifact is not yet present
+
+### Stakeholder Acceptance
+
+Status: adequate for moving into Epic 3 planning
+
+Evidence:
+
+- Epic 2 work is aligned with the approved PRD, UX, and architecture documents
+- no unresolved story-level review findings remain in the Epic 2 artifacts
+
+### Technical Health
+
+Status: solid, with a clear watch area
+
+Healthy areas:
+
+- canonical snapshot and presenter architecture
+- localized trust and degradation handling
+- stable live-reading behavior under updates
+
+Watch area:
+
+- negative-path honesty remains the highest-risk regression area and should stay under active review
+
+### Unresolved Blockers
+
+No blockers in the repo artifacts prevent Epic 3 from starting.
+
+## Commitments And Next Steps
+
+- Start Epic 3 with the existing canonical readiness and source-status model as the single source of truth.
+- Carry forward the boundary-truth review pattern that proved valuable in Epic 2.
+- Keep negative-path regression coverage mandatory, especially for denied access, unavailable sources, and recovery flows.
+- Use this retrospective as the baseline continuity document for the next epic.
+
+## Closing Summary
+
+Epic 2 was successful because it added live trust, disruption, degradation, and update continuity without breaking the product’s calm public identity. The main lesson is not that the team needs a new architecture; it is that truthful fallback behavior and canonical semantics are now the core quality bar for the project. Epic 3 is ready to proceed, provided those rules stay explicit.
